From f0cbec1c68efbe606e2063e8b2e0b09dd73123db Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 13:05:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] kanban: worker evict keeps origin-adopted copies;
 one stuck copy never aborts the pickup

worker-4 failed every worker-node pickup from 2026-11-13 11:09Z (`kanban: worker-node pickup
failed`, ~every 15 s): `evict()` picked an archived foreign copy (t_01a1d48abbb62104, created on
the macbook) that had been adopted on worker-4 with `kanban_origin_adopt`. Its
`origin_creation_intents` row (`task_id REFERENCES tasks(id)`, no ON DELETE) made
`DELETE FROM tasks` raise `FOREIGN KEY constraint failed`; the whole eviction transaction rolled
back and `pickup()` raised before importing anything, so cards pinned `machine:worker-4`
(t_01a1c7a649c59bec) were never imported.

Five whys:
1. Pinned cards not imported -> `pickup()` raised in `evict(conn)` before the import step.
2. evict raised -> DELETE FROM tasks hit an FK child (origin_creation_intents) it does not clear.
3. It reached that copy -> the evictable-copy query only exempts claimed/running/wake-probe copies,
   not a copy this gateway owns as an origin owner (an adoption writes a machine-local intent row
   onto an existing foreign copy).
4. One copy stopped every eviction and the pickup -> all copies are deleted in one write txn and
   pickup has no failure boundary around evict.

Intent semantics (decision): the intent row is the owner's origin-binding custody. It is
machine-local and never synced; its `binding_id` is a random secret. The hub collector publishes a
card's `origin_bindings` only from this table, and `kanban_origin_ledger.observe_owner_task`
checks the published bindings against it and advances/releases the origin chat's obligation only
from an owner record that still carries the marker plus the card's completion events and runs
(`kanban_terminal_carry._owner_proof` reads the same rows). Nothing in core ever deletes an intent
row. Evicting a terminal copy (deleting the intent too) would therefore strand the origin's
obligation exactly when the owner is about to publish the release. So the copy stays in every
unclaimed state, terminal included, the same way `kanban_placement_recovery` already exempts
intent-bearing cards. The carry module restores intent rows only when the binding owner is this
gateway, so an intent row here always means "this gateway owns the binding".

Fix:
- `evict()` excludes copies that have an `origin_creation_intents` row.
- Each copy is deleted in its own savepoint (`write_txn(allow_nested=True)`); a copy that still
  cannot go (any FK child the list does not know) is kept and logged by task id, and the other
  evictions commit.
- `pickup()` logs and continues when `evict()` raises, so imports always run.

Proof:
- RED on main's source: all 6 new tests fail; 5 with the production error
  `sqlite3.IntegrityError: FOREIGN KEY constraint failed` at kanban_worker_node.py:364, the
  sixth with `kanban: worker-node pickup failed`.
- GREEN: tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_evict_origin_intent.py 6/6; 100 related files (every test
  importing kanban_worker_node or calling evict) 2420 passed; the one failure,
  test_eviction_receipt_and_rollback_are_local_only, asserted the old raise and now asserts the
  refused copy is kept with its receipt rolled back.
- Mutants (sdlc-review mutants.py), 4/4 killed: keep clause dropped; per-copy catch dropped;
  savepoint removed with catch kept; pickup try around evict dropped.

Card: t_01a1d4f144e2f2a0
